%[text] ## What are Edges, and Why are Edges Important?
%[text] An edge is a boundary of connected pixels between two regions. Edges are crucial in computer vision and image processing for extracting information, recognizing objects, and recovering geometry and viewpoints from images. Edge detection is the initial step in segmenting images, particularly in simple scenes, to identify strong edges to extract useful information and transition from the original image to a processed one that includes outlines of objects.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Image of a set of colorful gears before and after edge detection.](text:image:3f99)
%%
%[text] ## Where do Edges Come From? 
%[text] Edges arise as a result of discontinuities in a variety of image properties, including surface normal, depth, surface color, and illumination. 
%[text] - **Surface normal discontinuities** occur when there are sudden changes in surface orientation. For example, if you look at the [cube below](internal:M_9714), at the edges where two faces meet, there is a discontinuous change in the direction of the surface normal. In everyday conversation, you would probably call this an edge.
%[text] - **Depth discontinuities** occur when there is an abrupt change in the distance between surfaces. If you look at the [cube below](internal:M_9714), the back edge of the cube is significantly closer to the viewer than the background of the image, thus representing a depth discontinuity.
%[text] - **Surface color discontinuities** occur when there is a change in color or intensity. Moving from the writing on the [cube below](internal:M_9714) to space on the cube without writing represents a surface color discontinuity.
%[text] - **Illumination discontinuities** occur when there is an abrupt change in lighting. You can see an example of the illumination discontinuity from the shadow of the [cube below](internal:M_9714). \
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_9714] ![Image of a 3D box with a W on the face. Arrows point to the Surface Normal, Depth, Surface Color, and Illumination discontinuities.](text:image:7bf3) 
%[text] ## Characterizing Edges From Image Profiles (1D)
%[text] Each pixel in an image has an intensity value, and an edge is defined by an abrupt change in this intensity. By visualizing these edges and their corresponding image profiles, you can analyze the difficulty of identifying edges from these profiles. Simplified 1D analysis, which examines intensity changes along a single line of pixels, helps us understand basic edge properties without the complexity of 2D data.
%[text] There are four common edge models in images: step, ramp, line, and roof edges.
%[text] - **Step edges** occur when a light region is adjacent to a dark region.
%[text] - **Line edges** occur when a thin line of light or dark occurs on a dark or light background.
%[text] - **Roof edges** occur when the lightness rapidly increases and then decreases, or vice versa.
%[text] - **Ramp edges** occur when the lightness gradually changes across a region. \
%[text] ![](text:image:70c2) **Try**. Visualize the ideal edges in 2D and their corresponding image intensity profiles in 1D.
%[text] 1. *Select one of the four ideal edges from the drop-down menu below.*
%[text] 2. *Use the marker to select a segment that spans both the light and dark areas of the image.*
%[text] 3. *Repeat this process for each of the four ideal edges.* \
IdealEdge = "Step.jpg"; % Non-default options include: "Step.jpg", "Ramp.jpg", "Line.jpg" and "Roof.jpg" %[control:dropdown:543e]{"position":[13,23]}
if ~strcmp(IdealEdge,"Select")
    [Position,EdgeImage,ImageTitle] = GetPosition(IdealEdge,false); %#ok<*UNRCH>

    DrawProf(IdealEdge,EdgeImage,ImageTitle,Position);
else
    warning("Select a valid option.")
end
%[text] ![](text:image:48da) **Reflect**. Compare and contrast the intensity profiles for the ideal images. 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_6c3f] ### Detecting Edges Amid Noise
%[text] We have been looking at nearly perfect ideal edges, but what happens when introducing noise? In this section, you will evaluate the 1D intensity profiles from 2D images of noisy edges.
%[text] ![](text:image:45af) **Try**. Visualize the ideal edges with noise in 2D and their corresponding image intensity profiles in 1D.
%[text] 1. *Select one of the four ideal edges from the drop-down menu below.*
%[text] 2. *Use the marker to select a segment from the light to dark region of the image.*
%[text] 3. *Repeat this process for each of the four ideal edges.* \
IdealEdge = "Ramp.jpg"; % Non-default options include: "Step.jpg", "Ramp.jpg", "Line.jpg" and "Roof.jpg" %[control:dropdown:69e7]{"position":[13,23]}
if ~strcmp(IdealEdge,"Select")
    [Position,EdgeImage,ImageTitle] = GetPosition(IdealEdge,true);
    DrawProf(IdealEdge,EdgeImage,ImageTitle,Position);
else
    warning("Select a valid option.")
end
%[text] ![](text:image:3f6f) **Reflect**. Compare and contrast the difficulty level of identifying the edge amid noise from the intensity profiles. 
%%
%[text] ### Using the First Derivative to Detect Edges (1D)
%[text] In calculus, the derivative of a continuous function represents its rate of change. Although intensity profiles are discrete rather than continuous, we can still compute a numerical derivative. In the numerical derivative of an intensity profile, sharp changes in intensity correspond to peaks of the first derivative. Sharp peaks correspond to strong edges, and gradual peaks correspond to weak edges.
%[text] ![](text:image:8dc0) **Try**. Visualize the derivative of the ideal edges and quantify the number of sharp peaks (or edges).
%[text] 1. *Select one of the four ideal edges from the drop-down menu below.*
%[text] 2. *Use the button to visualize the first derivative of the intensity profile of your chosen edge.*
%[text] 3. *Repeat this process for each of the four ideal edges.* \
option = "Line Edge"; % Non-default options include: "Step Edge", "Ramp Edge", "Line Edge" and "Roof Edge" %[control:dropdown:99ba]{"position":[10,21]}
tSignal = linspace(-5,5,500);
PlotEdge(option,tSignal,"Derivative","WithDrawNow")
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:9e93) **Exercise 1.** 
%[text] For which case(s) of the idealized edges does the 1D derivative-based edge-finding identify weak edges? 
%[text] **Solution:**
CheckAnswer("Exercise1",[false true false true])% [step ramp line roof] %[control:checkbox:058f]{"position":[26,31]} %[control:checkbox:4b2c]{"position":[32,36]} %[control:checkbox:3ab2]{"position":[37,42]} %[control:checkbox:22e5]{"position":[43,47]}
  %[control:button:1dd8]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:45ef) **Exercise 2.** Based on the number of sharp peaks in the plot of the first derivative of the function, how many strong edges are present in the plot below?
%[text] 
%[text] ![Graph of the first derivative of an intensity function vs. Position. The graph has 5 sharp peaks: 3 positive peaks and 2 negative peaks.](text:image:3dc0)
%[text] **Solution:**
CheckAnswer("Exercise2",5) %[control:editfield:7cd8]{"position":[25,26]}
  %[control:button:3b4a]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ### Using the First Derivative to Detect Edges Amid Noise (1D)
%[text] Recall from the [Detecting Edges Amid Noise](internal:M_6c3f) section above that when we add noise the edge is more difficult to detect than in the original function. With noise, we have a lot of sharp peaks, but we want to distinguish between the true edges and the noise. To do this, we need to understand the concept of prominence, which measures how much a peak stands out from the surrounding data. This measurement is crucial for identifying significant edges. Let's look at the derivative of the 1D function and quantify the number of sharp peaks. 
%[text]  ![](text:image:82c8) **Try**. Visualize the derivative of the ideal edges amid noise and quantify the number of sharp peaks (or edges).
%[text] 1. *Select one of the four ideal edges from the drop-down menu below.*
%[text] 2. *Use the button to visualize the first derivative of the intensity profile of your chosen edge.*
%[text] 3. *Repeat this process for each of the four ideal edges.* \
%[text] ![Warning symbol](text:image:1953)  The threshold for noise has been adjusted to enable visualization.
option = "Step Edge"; % Non-default options include: "Step Edge", "Ramp Edge", "Line Edge" and "Roof Edge" %[control:dropdown:964b]{"position":[10,21]}
if option == "Select"
    warning("Select a valid option.");
    return
end
tSignal = linspace(-5,5,500);
NoisySignal = GenNoisySig(option,tSignal);
df = CalcDeriv(NoisySignal);

% Plot the original and noisy signals for comparison
plot(tSignal,CreateBaseSignal(option,tSignal),DisplayName="Original Signal",SeriesIndex=1)
hold on
plot(tSignal,NoisySignal,DisplayName="Noisy Signal",SeriesIndex=2)
hold off
legend(Location="bestoutside")
title("Original and Noisy Signals")
xlabel("Position")
ylabel("Intensity of Signal")

% Plot the derivative of the noisy signal
plot(tSignal(1:end-1),df,SeriesIndex=2,DisplayName="Derivative")
Threshold = 0.5;
[locsMax2,maxPeak2,locsMin2,minPeak2] = FindPeaks(df,Threshold); % Identify peak locations

% Plot the peaks
hold on
plot(tSignal(locsMax2),maxPeak2,"^",DisplayName="Positive Peak",SeriesIndex=3);
plot(tSignal(locsMin2),-minPeak2,"v",DisplayName="Negative Peak",SeriesIndex=4);
hold off

pks = nnz(maxPeak2)+nnz(minPeak2); % Calculate the total number of peaks

title("First Derivative of "+newline+option+" Noisy Signal")
xlabel("Position")
ylabel("First Derivative of Intensity Signal")
axis padded
legend(Location="bestoutside");
disp("Number of sharp peaks: "+pks)
%%
%[text] ### Handling Noise in Edge Detection (1D)
%[text] We can smooth the noise by convolving a Gaussian filter with the original signal. Notice that as you increase the smoothing factor, you minimize the noise, but the edge becomes less prominent and more difficult to detect. 
%[text] ![](text:image:2f1c) **Try**. Smooth noise using a Gaussian filter.
%[text] 1. *Adjust the smoothing factor value to smooth the signal to eliminate noise and quantify the number of sharp peaks for the smoothed signal.*
%[text] 2. *Can you get the number of sharp peaks to match the ideal signal without noise?* \
option = "Ramp Edge"; % Non-default options include: "Step Edge", "Ramp Edge", "Line Edge" and "Roof Edge" %[control:dropdown:1853]{"position":[10,21]}
if option == "Select" %#ok<*BDSCI>
    warning("Select a valid option.");
    return
end
tSignal2 = linspace(-5,5,500);
NoisySignal = GenNoisySig(option,tSignal2);
df2 = CalcDeriv(NoisySignal);

% Smooth input signal
[SmoothedData,winSize] = smoothdata(NoisySignal,"gaussian",SmoothingFactor=0.05);  %[control:slider:3567]{"position":[76,80]} %[control:button:1993]{"position":[82,83]}
dfSmooth = diff(SmoothedData);
dfSmooth = dfSmooth/max(dfSmooth);

plot(tSignal2(1:end-1),df2,SeriesIndex=2,DisplayName="Noisy Derivative") % Plot original Derivative
hold on

% Find local maxima and minima
maxIndices3 = islocalmax(dfSmooth,MinProminence=1);
minIndices3 = islocalmin(dfSmooth,MinProminence=1);

% Display results
plot(tSignal2(1:end-1),dfSmooth,SeriesIndex=6,DisplayName="Smoothed Signal")
hold on

% Plot local maxima
scatter(tSignal2(maxIndices3),dfSmooth(maxIndices3),"^","filled",SeriesIndex=2,DisplayName="Positive Peak")

% Plot local minima
scatter(tSignal2(minIndices3),dfSmooth(minIndices3),"v","filled",SeriesIndex=3,DisplayName="Negative Peak")

title("First Derivative of Smoothed "+newline+option+ " Signal")
xlabel("Position")
ylabel("First Derivative of"+newline+"Intensity Signal")
axis padded
legend(Location="bestoutside")
hold off
pks = nnz(maxIndices3)+nnz(minIndices3); % Calculate the total number of peaks
disp("Number of sharp peak(s): " + pks)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:TMP_04ae] ![](text:image:7056) **Application.** Use the first derivative to detect edges to identify the elbow bone in the X-ray image.
  %[control:button:08ee]{"position":[1,2]}
AppIm = "XRayofArm.jpg";
[Position,EdgeImage,ImageTitle] = GetPosition(AppIm,false);
DrawProf(AppIm,EdgeImage,ImageTitle,Position);
%%
%[text] ### Using the Second Derivative to Detect Edges (1D)
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_8280] In calculus, the second derivative measures how the rate of change of a function changes. For 1D signals, it reflects curvature or slope changes. Edges can be detected at [zero-crossings](internal:M_8500), where the second derivative changes sign or crosses zero.
%[text] ![](text:image:3aff) **Try**. Visualize the second derivative of the ideal edges and quantify the number of **zero-crossings** (or edges).
option = "Roof Edge"; % Non-default options include: "Step Edge", "Ramp Edge", "Line Edge" and "Roof Edge" %[control:dropdown:7c3c]{"position":[10,21]}

PlotEdge(option,tSignal,"SecondDerivative","WithDrawNow")
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:19ab) **Exercise 3.** Based on the number of **zero-crossings** in the plot of the second derivative of the function, how many edges are present?
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Graph of the Second derivative of the intensity profile vs. the position. There are 7 Zero-crossings in the image. ](text:image:0bfc)
%[text] **Solution:**
CheckAnswer("Exercise3",7);  %[control:editfield:949a]{"position":[25,26]} %[control:button:59b7]{"position":[28,29]}
%%
%[text] ## Edge Detection by Gradients (2D)
%[text] We have used simplified 1D analysis to understand the basic location of edges and qualitatively determine the strength. Now, we'll focus on obtaining a more complete and accurate representation of images by quantifying the edge gradient vector, which provides both [edge strength](internal:M_0847) and [edge direction](internal:M_4574). Since we typically work with 2D images, we don't usually analyze them from a 1D perspective of individual pixels or intensities.
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_1c62] Mathematically, for a function $f(x,y)${"altText":"Function f of variables x and y."} the [gradient vector ](internal:M_7e81),$\\nabla f${"altText":"Gradient of the function f."}, is given by:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\text{gradient vector} =\\nabla f =\\left\[ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} , \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\right\]\n\n\n\n\n${"altText":"The gradient vector of f is equal to the partial derivatives of f with respect to x and y."}
%[text] The [gradient vector](internal:M_7e81) of a 2D image represents the direction and rate of the most rapid intensity change at each pixel. It is composed of two components:
%[text] 1. gradient in the x-direction $\\displaystyle \\left(\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\right)\n\n\n\n\n\n${"altText":"Partial derivative of f with respect to x."}: This measures the change in intensity along the horizontal axis.
%[text] 2. gradient in the y-direction $\\displaystyle \\left(\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\right)\n\n\n\n\n\n${"altText":"Partial derivative of f with respect to y."}: This measures the change in intensity along the vertical axis. \
%[text] In the image below, you will see the gradient vector drawn on the **ramp edge**. Notice the impact of gradient changes in the x and y-direction on the images. 
%[text:table]
%[text] | **Gradient** | **Changes in**  | **Visualization** |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | $\\nabla f =\\left\[ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} , \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\right\]\n\n\n\n\n${"altText":"The gradient of f is equal to the vector of partial derivatives with respect to x and y."} | any direction | ![Image of a gradient moving towards the top right. There is an arrow indicating the direction of the gradient.](text:image:67ac) |
%[text] | $\\nabla f = \\left\[ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} , 0 \\right\]\n${"altText":"Gradient of f with partial derivative with respect to x and zero for y."} | only in x | ![Image of a gradient moving from left to right. There is an arrow indicating the direction of the gradient.](text:image:91ca) |
%[text] | $\\nabla f = \\left\[ 0 , \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\right\]\n${"altText":"Gradient of f with zero for x and partial derivative with respect to y."} | only in y | ![Image of a gradient moving from top to bottom. There is an arrow indicating the direction of the gradient.](text:image:6f3e) |
%[text:table]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_90c0] The [edge strength ](internal:M_0847)is given by the magnitude of the [gradient vector](internal:M_7e81). A larger gradient magnitude signifies a steeper change at the edge, while a smaller magnitude suggests a more gradual change. For a **ramp edge**, the gradient might be less pronounced compared to a sharp edge, reflecting a gradual change in intensity. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\text{edge strength}  = |\\nabla f| = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\right)^2}\n${"altText":"Edge strength is equal to the magnitude of the gradient of f, calculated as the square root of the sum of the squares of the partial derivatives with respect to x and y."}
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_0639] The [edge direction](internal:M_4574) is given by the direction of the [gradient vector](internal:M_7e81), $\\theta${"altText":"The lowercase Greek symbol, theta."}, and points in the direction of the steepest intensity change. For a **step edge**, this direction is perpendicular to the edge itself. A large **edge direction** indicates a sharp transition between different areas in the image, such as the line between a dark object and a light background. In contrast, a small **edge direction** signifies a gradual transition between regions of lightness.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\text{edge direction}=\\theta = \\tan^{-1} { \~\~\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\~\~  \\overwithdelims () {\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} }\n${"altText":"Edge direction is equal to the angle \theta, calculated as the inverse tangent of the ratio of the partial derivatives of f with respect to y and x."}
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:04b6) **Try**. Compare the [edge strength](internal:M_0847) for the **step edge** and the **ramp edge**. 
%[text] 1. *Select an ideal edge image from the drop-down menu below.*
%[text] 2. *Use your mouse to draw a horizontal line across white to black regions of the image.*  \
Image = "Ramp.jpg"; % Non-default options include "Step.jpg" and "Ramp.jpg" %[control:dropdown:7672]{"position":[9,19]}
if ~strcmp(Image,"Select")
    [Position,EdgeImage,ImageTitle] = GetPosition(Image,false); %#ok<*ASGLU>
    imshow(Image)
    hold on
    [Mag,Dir] = DrawGrad(Image,Position,"Static",330);
    title("The edge strength is "+round(Mag))
    hold off
else
    warning("Select a valid option.")
end
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:952b) **Reflect**.The gradient vector points in the direction of the fastest increase in intensity. How does the gradient relate to the direction of the edge?
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:3c9a) **Application.** In medical imaging, diseases are often detected when clear edges are not visible. A weak edge might indicate disease, while a strong edge suggests health. Remember, the stronger the edge, the higher the likelihood of clear edge detection. Compare the [edge strength](internal:M_0847) in the two lung images below and determine which lung is more likely to be diseased.
%[text] ![](text:image:2058) **Demonstration**. Calculate the edge strength for the first lung image.
  %[control:button:41c7]{"position":[1,2]}
ImgApp1 = "LungA.jpg";
imshow(ImgApp1)
% drawnow
hold on
Position1 = [185,608;185,558];
[Mag1,Dir1] = DrawGrad(ImgApp1,Position1,"DrawNow",640);
hold off
title("The edge strength is "+round(Mag1))
disp(("Image Source: Mikael Häggström, M.D."+newline+"CC0, via Wikimedia Commons"+newline+sprintf('<a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Normal_posteroanterior_(PA)_chest_radiograph_(X-ray).jpg">X-Ray of Chest</a>')))
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:6c7d) **Try**. Calculate the edge strength for the second lung image.
%[text] 1. *Draw a vertical vector up the rib cage using the marker.* \
  %[control:button:9730]{"position":[1,2]}
ImgApp2 = "LungB.jpg";
imshow(ImgApp2)
[Position2,ImgApp2,ImageTitle] = GetPosition(ImgApp2,false);
imshow(ImgApp2)
hold on
[Mag2,Dir2] = DrawGrad(ImgApp2,Position2,"Static",640);
title("The edge strength is "+round(Mag2))
hold off
disp("Image Source: Mikael Häggström, M.D."+newline+...
    "Consent note: Written informed consent was obtained from the individual, including online publication.,"+newline+...
    "CC0, via Wikimedia Commons"+newline+...
    sprintf('<a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chest_X-ray_in_influenza_and_Haemophilus_influenzae.jpg">X-Ray of Chest</a>'))
%%
%[text] ## Glossary
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_4574] **edge direction**: the orientation or angle of an edge in an image. It indicates the direction in which the intensity changes most rapidly, helping to understand the structure and shape of objects within the image. [(↑ Return to Text)](internal:M_0639)
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_0847] **edge strength**: the measure of the intensity or sharpness of a boundary between different regions in an image. It quantifies how distinct or pronounced the transition is from one area to another. [(↑ Return to Text)](internal:M_90c0)
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_7e81] **gradient vector:** a vector that points in the direction of the greatest rate of increase of a function. [(↑ Return to Text)](internal:M_1c62)
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_8500] **zero-crossings**: the points in a signal or function where the value changes sign, crossing from positive to negative or vice versa. [(↑ Return to Text)](internal:M_8280)
